#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7149ac-2bbd-5157-b847-7e20a49ac8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=-kM,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310449} {:answer/id #uuid "a240a70d-4182-51c1-9562-8bd784e6db5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{M},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310450} {:answer/id #uuid "204adecc-48ac-589a-a2c4-c45b2bb9c566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=kM,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310451} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf19db2-ca38-5089-b7d8-8b06876fecbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=k+M,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310452} {:answer/id #uuid "d6aadcb2-900d-53bc-b91a-9e4af7e70d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=-k{M}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "204adecc-48ac-589a-a2c4-c45b2bb9c566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}\\text{M}}{\\text{d}t}=kM,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310451} :answer-field/id #uuid "c61bbc42-877d-5f75-b824-673922e2d663" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa32ad2d-05af-5067-9118-2c0c283b8d2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119054" :question/problem "The amount of money $M(t)$ in a saving account increases at a rate that is proportional to the amount of money currently in the account. What differential equation describes the rate of change in the amount of money in the saving account $M,$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **amount of money** $M(t)$ in a saving account **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **amount of money** currently in the saving account.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *amount of money* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *amount of money.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}∝M.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the amount of money *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=kM.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68b74365-7af7-533c-a4b6-82b95ad8c598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295869} {:answer/id #uuid "4dac0845-ece5-5c93-ac78-e3107df5c303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295870} {:answer/id #uuid "978ca961-fc0f-5ab9-9bc4-26f6aa707375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295871} {:answer/id #uuid "b49dba43-1fc2-53b2-b7f9-3b4142fab53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295872} {:answer/id #uuid "3e965958-eaee-5339-9cf7-a5c18c0224a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "978ca961-fc0f-5ab9-9bc4-26f6aa707375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295871} :answer-field/id #uuid "b79b0e9e-ce07-5367-ae03-a5fb5855bf88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "116a80ae-5b7a-5a13-919a-314322a246f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-143035" :question/problem "During a certain chemical reaction, the volume of gas $G$ increases with time. The rate of change of the volume of gas is proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds, since the reaction started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of gas at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a certain chemical reaction, the **volume** of gas $G$ **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the volume of gas is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of gas* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of gas *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9040d995-4b91-5e52-95f2-d5cb882a3dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k{M}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310456} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c509b0-e6da-5199-994a-ce81c59fc86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k{M}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310457} {:answer/id #uuid "82327f25-b37f-5b73-a9c3-1e5026b7b754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-kM,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310458} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b2ef34-4c74-5b5f-b815-305fd9653851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k\\sqrt{M},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310459} {:answer/id #uuid "22c53dcf-4d03-528b-b10d-afdce686e0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{M}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2c509b0-e6da-5199-994a-ce81c59fc86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k{M}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310457} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1b0ad4-b3f0-58ba-847e-112cee74785f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b29e13ab-6240-5953-8f63-4daf76f87654" :question/math-academy-id "q-143038" :question/problem "During a chemical reaction, the mass $M$ of a particular substance decreases with time. The rate of change of the substance's mass is proportional to the square of the mass. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the substance's mass at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a chemical reaction, the **mass** $M$ of a particular substance  **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the substance's mass is **proportional** to the **square of the mass**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *mass* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *mass squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}∝{M}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the mass *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k{M}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aefdcf67-bcdc-5ed9-b0c0-b392a37658bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295883} {:answer/id #uuid "08fff3d5-f006-5e51-b487-252567288d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-kH,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb91c8b-41a8-5201-a728-dda49efb372f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kH,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295885} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e1903b-8dd1-5d3e-990b-7e0192bf2bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295886} {:answer/id #uuid "ce285e3c-2f8f-5986-9c6a-2c74be616204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aefdcf67-bcdc-5ed9-b0c0-b392a37658bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295883} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9392e9a-f656-5daf-b678-018e74b9f8bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91ea1276-29dd-5d34-8bf4-312e105d7100" :question/math-academy-id "q-143058" :question/problem "The height $H$ of a tree increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the tree's height at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height $H?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** $H$ of a tree **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **tree's height** $H$ at that moment. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *tree's height.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{H}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8655d0d5-55e3-52ac-89f2-c3711a78f077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{k}{V}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126405} {:answer/id #uuid "bab709a9-cc87-5894-bb68-68ea87bab745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{k}{V^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126406} {:answer/id #uuid "dc93eadb-9170-5cbc-9a40-c31c3a50cd57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{k}{D^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126407} {:answer/id #uuid "35f11a78-7261-5f94-b60d-90fb748ec7b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kD^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126408} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7caeff-d075-5041-825f-03ebd1f8337e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-\\frac{k}{V^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bab709a9-cc87-5894-bb68-68ea87bab745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{k}{V^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126406} :answer-field/id #uuid "3488303e-9616-521b-9d40-6741a6414854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32ecd42c-1a16-5fd5-b3e0-a97998e936b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143076" :question/problem "The volume $V$ of a balloon being inflated increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of the balloon?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume $V$ of a balloon** being inflated **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the volume**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$"}]}
